    "result_text": "The 11 test classes (all `Cp01`\u2013`Cp11`) all ran \u2014 21 tests, green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` behavior on `POST /api/owners`.\n\nThe rejection + opt-in half (`sharesHousehold` true allows a same-lastName owner at an already-used address) was already present in `CheckHouseholdUnique`. The new requirement was to **assign and return a stable shared `householdId`**. Changes:\n\n- **`AssignHouseholdId`** (new function, `rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 derives `householdId` as the first 12 upper-case hex chars of `SHA-256(\"<lastName>|<address>\")`, using the *same* normalization (lower-case, collapse whitespace, trim) as `CheckHouseholdUnique`. Because it's a pure function of the normalized last name + address, two owners who share a household deterministically get the identical id with no lookup or mutation of the existing owner \u2014 \"stable\" and \"shared\" by construction.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the pipeline after `assignCustomerCode` and before `save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`, so the id is persisted and returned.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `household_id` column with getter/setter (mirroring `customerCode`).\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `householdId` as a read-only property on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it (and the MapStruct mapper carries it through on response). `OwnerFields` is unchanged, keeping `sharesHousehold` write-only.\n- **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 ignore `householdId` when mapping the request `OwnerFieldsDto` \u2192 entity (it's assigned by the function step, not the body).\n- **DB schemas** \u2014 added `household_id` to the owners table in all four dialects (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres).\n\nThe new `Cp11Tests.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` passes (two `sharesHousehold` joiners get an equal, non-blank `householdId`), `Cp10` still passes, and the full suite is green (21/21).",
